# Global Rules

## Communication

- **Parse terse input** — understand typos, abbreviations ("impl", "curr", "pls"), voice transcription, and single-word commands without asking for clarification on obvious intent.
- **Brief narration** — one sentence of context, then results. No preamble essays or "Let me start by..." introductions.
- **Short affirmatives = proceed** — "ok", "go", "impl", "yes", "." all mean start implementing immediately.

## Workflow

- **"push" = verify + commit + push** — run uncovered checks per the verify skill (skip anything the pre-push hook already runs), commit with a descriptive message, then push and let the hook validate the rest. Don't ask what to include.
- **"examine X" = explore, don't act** — come back with findings and assessment. Never start modifying code when asked to examine something.
- **Refactors preserve behavior** — when refactoring, verify existing functionality is unchanged. Feature work may intentionally change behavior.

## Code & Docs

- **DRY documentation** — update CLAUDE.md, README, tech debt, and notes on architectural changes. Never duplicate info across doc files — reference instead.
- **Run verification before declaring done** — uncovered checks per the verify skill. Fix any issues you introduced.
- **TDD where appropriate** — for non-trivial logic, write failing tests first, then implement to make them pass. Skip for glue code, config, or trivial changes where tests add no signal.

## Quality

- **Assume LLM review** — your output (including uncommited code) will be reviewed by a consortium of LLMs after implementation. Write code and comments that are clear, well-structured, and defensible under automated scrutiny. 

## Implementation Assumptions

When implementing non-trivial code, report any significant assumptions or design decisions made without explicit user direction. At the end of implementation, include a **"Assumptions & Decisions"** section with bullet points covering:

- **What** — the decision or assumption made
- **Where** — which file(s) / area it affects
- **Why** — reasoning behind the choice
- **Alternatives** — what you'd change if the user disagrees

"Significant" means choices that affect behavior, data shape, API surface, naming conventions, architectural patterns, or scope boundaries — not routine implementation details like variable names or loop structure. Use best judgment.

## Never

- **Never commit or push** without explicit permission in a separate user message.
- **Never backfill or migrate existing data** unless explicitly asked.
